# 8. SECTION FOUR — UNIVERSITETLAR

## Objective

Transform the Universities section into a genuinely comprehensive global university search engine.

This is one of the most important parts of the entire application.

The user must be able to discover universities even when they do not know the exact official spelling.

### 8.1 Comprehensive university database

Expand the university database as much as reliable data sources allow.

Include:

- Internationally recognized universities.
- National universities.
- Public universities.
- Private universities.
- Technical universities.
- Research universities.
- Specialized institutions.
- Recognized universities outside the global top rankings.
- Regional universities.
- Universities in less frequently searched countries.

Do not make global ranking position a condition for inclusion.

A university should not be excluded merely because it is not internationally famous.

However, distinguish universities from colleges, vocational schools, academies, and other higher-education institutions where the distinction matters.

Do not falsely label every educational institution as a university.

### 8.2 Advanced intelligent search

The university search must be significantly more flexible.

The user may search using:

- Full official name.
- Partial university name.
- Acronym.
- Alternative name.
- Local-language name.
- English name.
- City.
- Country.
- Common transliteration.
- Minor spelling mistakes.
- Missing letters.
- Extra letters.
- Incorrect word spacing.
- Approximate pronunciation-based spelling where technically feasible.

For example, the system should be able to recognize searches that are close to:

- "Tokyo University".
- "The University of Tokyo".
- "Tokyo daigaku".
- "Univercity of Tokyo".
- "Tokio university".
- "Kyutech".
- "Kyushu Institute of Technology".
- "Kyushu Institute of Technlogy".

These are illustrative examples of search flexibility.

The system should recognize likely intended universities and display suggestions.

### 8.3 Search architecture

Do not implement fuzzy search using a single simplistic frontend condition.

Use a layered search strategy:

**Layer 1 — Exact matching**

Search official names and known aliases.

**Layer 2 — Prefix and partial matching**

Find universities when the user types only part of a name.

**Layer 3 — Fuzzy matching**

Handle common spelling errors and minor typos.

**Layer 4 — Alternative-name matching**

Search local-language names, abbreviations, transliterations, and known alternative names.

**Layer 5 — Geographic matching**

Allow searches by city and country.

**Layer 6 — Intelligent suggestions**

When the exact university is not found, show likely matching institutions.

Use suitable search infrastructure according to the existing backend.

Possible technologies include:

- PostgreSQL full-text search.
- PostgreSQL trigram similarity.
- Dedicated search engines where justified.
- Indexed aliases.
- Unicode normalization.
- Language-aware tokenization.

Do not introduce an unnecessarily complicated search stack if the existing database can support these features efficiently.

### 8.4 Search experience

The search should provide:

- Fast results.
- Suggestions while typing.
- Clear university names.
- City and country labels.
- Search result highlighting.
- Relevant ranking information where available.
- Filters.
- Sorting.
- Clear no-result messages.
- Typo-tolerant suggestions.

If the user enters a misspelled name, do not immediately show an empty screen.

Instead, display:

**"Siz quyidagini nazarda tutdingizmi?"**

Then show the most relevant possible matches.

Do not silently redirect to a university when multiple matches are plausible.

