# 13. DATABASE ARCHITECTURE & PERFORMANCE

A large database requires proper infrastructure.

Do not simply place thousands of records into frontend arrays.

### 13.1 Database design

Inspect and improve the existing schema.

Use normalized relationships where appropriate.

Potential entities include:

- Countries.
- Cities.
- Universities.
- UniversityAliases.
- UniversityLocations.
- AcademicPrograms.
- DegreeLevels.
- AdmissionRequirements.
- Scholarships.
- ScholarshipEligibility.
- Rankings.
- Certificates.
- EducationCosts.
- DataSources.
- DataVerificationHistory.

Adapt names and structure to the existing database.

Avoid creating duplicate entities if equivalent tables already exist.

### 13.2 Indexing

Create suitable indexes for frequently used search fields.

Consider:

- University official name.
- University aliases.
- Country ID.
- City ID.
- Geographic coordinates.
- Scholarship provider.
- Degree level.
- Academic field.

Use database indexing appropriate to the existing technology.

### 13.3 Pagination and loading

For large datasets:

- Use server-side pagination.
- Use efficient filtering.
- Use progressive loading.
- Use caching where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary repeated requests.
- Avoid loading the entire global database into browser memory.

The user should still be able to search the entire dataset even when only one page of results is currently visible.

### 13.4 Duplicate detection

Detect duplicate universities and other records using:

- Official identifiers.
- Website domains.
- Known aliases.
- Country and city.
- Name similarity.
- External dataset identifiers.

Do not merge records automatically when the match is uncertain.

Keep a review mechanism for ambiguous duplicates.

### 13.5 Data update strategy

Where possible, support:

- Incremental updates.
- Scheduled synchronization.
- Manual refresh.
- Failed-job logging.
- Source tracking.
- Update timestamps.
- Duplicate prevention.

Do not overwrite user-created content.

